You are working on a NestJS project using TypeScript.

Follow ALL rules below strictly.

# Core Principles

- Follow SOLID principles:
  - Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
  - Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
  - Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
- Apply DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Apply KISS (Keep It Simple)
- Apply YAGNI (You Aren’t Gonna Need It)
- Prefer readability over cleverness
- Code must be self-explanatory

# Architecture

- Use modular architecture (feature-based)
- Organize code by domain, NOT by technical type
- Each module must represent a business domain

Example structure:
src/
  modules/
    users/
      users.module.ts
      users.controller.ts
      users.service.ts
      dto/
      entities/
  common/
  config/
  utils/

- Avoid large modules (split when needed)
- Business rules MUST NOT depend on NestJS framework

# Layers

- Controllers:
  - Handle only HTTP request/response
  - No business logic allowed

- Services:
  - Contain business logic

- Repositories/Providers:
  - Handle data access

- DTOs:
  - Handle validation and typing

# Dependency Injection

- Always use NestJS DI
- NEVER instantiate dependencies manually using "new"
- Use constructor injection

# TypeScript Rules

- Enable strict mode
- NEVER use "any"
- Always type:
  - variables
  - parameters
  - return values

- Prefer utility types:
  - Partial
  - Pick
  - Omit

# Clean Code

- Functions:
  - Max ~20 lines
  - Single responsibility
  - Use early returns

- Classes:
  - Max ~200 lines
  - Max ~10 public methods

# Naming Conventions

- Classes: PascalCase
- Variables/Functions: camelCase
- Files: kebab-case
- Environment variables: UPPER_CASE
- Booleans: prefix with is/has/can

# DTO and Validation

- Always use class-validator
- Never trust external input
- Always validate incoming data

# Imports (IMPORTANT)

- ALWAYS use project aliases (e.g. "~/", "@/")
- NEVER use relative paths like "../../"

Correct:
import { UsersService } from '~/modules/users/users.service';

Incorrect:
import { UsersService } from '../../../users/users.service';

# Business Logic

- Must be:
  - isolated
  - testable
  - framework-independent

- NEVER put business logic in:
  - controllers
  - decorators
  - interceptors

# Configuration

- Use @nestjs/config
- NEVER access process.env directly
- Centralize configuration

# Reusability

- Use common/ for shared components:
  - guards
  - interceptors
  - pipes
  - filters

- Avoid code duplication

# Testing

- Use Jest
- Follow Arrange / Act / Assert pattern

- Required:
  - Unit tests (services)
  - E2E tests (controllers)

# Best Practices

- Use interceptors for:
  - logging
  - response transformation

- Use pipes for validation
- Implement health check endpoint (/health)
- Use structured logging

# Anti-Patterns (FORBIDDEN)

- Business logic inside controllers
- Huge services
- Circular dependencies
- Usage of "any"
- Manual dependency instantiation
- Code duplication
- Disorganized global modules

# Golden Rule

If code is hard to test, it is probably badly structured.
